Start with the check itself, since everything else turns on it. Take the string, throw away what isn't a letter or a number, and compare it with its own reverse. In Perl that's about six lines.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use open qw(:std :utf8);

my $input = shift @ARGV or die "Usage: $0 <string>\n";

my $clean = $input;
$clean =~ s/\W//g;   # removes everything except letters and digits

if ($clean eq reverse $clean) {
    print "\"$input\" is a palindrome\n";
} else {
    print "\"$input\" is not a palindrome\n";
}
```

The `use utf8` and the two `open` lines are worth a word, because a missing one is the most common bug in a script like this. Without them, a file of Japanese characters, or a name with an accented letter in it, comes in as a string of bytes. The reverse works on bytes, so "été" becomes "été", which looks right to nobody. With them, it comes in as characters, and the reverse works on characters.

The quotes around `$input` in the output are there so that a sentence which ends in a palindrome shows where the palindrome ends. Without them, "racecar is not a palindrome" reads as if "racecar is not a" were the thing being tested.

Now the input. What happens with nothing at all? The empty string has nothing to reverse, and the script says it's a palindrome, which is technically true and not much use. Empty input is also the usual sign that a user typed nothing, so treat it as an error, and say so. A string with nothing but spaces in it passes, and should pass. "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" is the classic case. Each version of this comes out as "amanaplanacanalpanama", reversed, and the two are equal.

One more thing, because people come to this problem from different places. Some of them, English speakers among them, are taught that a palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards, and that it doesn't count if the letters don't match. So "racecar" counts, and "raceca" doesn't. Others, and some languages come closer to this than English does, treat a word and its reverse as two separate words that happen to sound alike. "Racecar" is one word. "Stressed" and "desserts" are two. The second view is a good one, held by thoughtful people, and there's a plain test for it. Take the first half of the string and see whether it equals the second half reversed. Run "desserts" through the script and it comes out as a palindrome, and the second half, "rtsessed", is a word. I'd leave that as a note beneath the output. It's true, and the user can decide whether it matters.

Now the second half of the request, which is a larger script. Someone asked for a file of strings, one per line, with the count of palindromes at the end. That's a real job, and the first version of it is about twenty lines. Read each line, strip its trailing newline, skip it if it's empty, check it, and count the yeses. Here is the version I'd write.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use open qw(:std :utf8);

my $count = 0;
my $n = 0;

while (my $line = <STDIN>) {
    chomp $line;
    next if $line =~ /^\s*$/;   # skip blank lines
    $n++;
    $count++ if is_palindrome($line);
}

print "$count of $n\n";

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($s) = @_;
    my $clean = $s;
    $clean =~ s/\W//g;
    return $clean eq reverse $clean;
}
```

The validation goes in the same way as before. Empty lines are skipped, and the count only covers the lines that had something in them. Each line is checked on its own. Nothing in one line depends on any other, so there's no hidden ordering in it.

Where it breaks is the size. A million short lines is fine. A few hundred megabytes of them and the count is the whole job, so it runs in about a minute. A single string of a few hundred million characters, such as a whole book, runs the script's own check on the whole thing, which means it holds the string and its reverse in memory at once. A file of that size is better checked one chunk at a time, comparing the first characters with the last, moving inward, and giving up at the first mismatch. Whether that's worth writing depends on how often the big case comes up. I'd write it if it came up. As far as I know it doesn't.

The one place where I'm unsure is the character class. `\W` is "not a word character", which in Perl means the opposite of `[A-Za-z0-9_]`. That underscore is the part I'd look at twice. A string like "hello_world" passes, and nobody has settled whether it should. I'd leave it in, because a string with no letters at all, such as "12321", should get the same answer as "abcba", and that's a fair argument. If someone has a strong view either way, it costs a few seconds to change one character.

The program below does what you asked for. Read the whole input before you start checking it. If the user types a bad regular expression, or nothing at all, the program tells them and asks again. It does not try to guess what they meant, and it does not count from nothing.

The first thing it checks is whether the input is empty, and it checks this before it does anything else. An empty string is a real case, and the program handles it by saying so plainly. The next thing it checks is whether the pattern is valid. It tests the pattern with Pattern.compile() inside a try block. If compiling fails, it catches that particular problem and prints the message from the exception, so the user learns what was wrong with their typing. If something else goes wrong, it prints a generic message and asks again. Further down is the loop over the lines. Each line is checked with a Matcher, and a line that fails to match simply gets skipped. If the program counted bad lines, or stopped on the first bad one, it would be wrong in a way the user could not see from outside.

The program counts only lines that match. If a block of text contains several matches on one line, the line counts as one. If you want every match counted, change the loop so that each match found increments the counter, and I would count that as a simpler loop. The program also tells the user where the matches are, on the same line as the count, so they can check it.

One thing is left to you. The program assumes the pattern is meant as a regular expression, because that is what you asked for. A user who typed "hello" may have meant the word hello, and a pattern that only matches lines containing "hello" is a fair guess. I am not sure of that, and some users would want the other behavior. So it says at the top what it does. If the program is for one person's own files, a search box with a regex toggle costs very little, and I would build it that way.

The program is written for Java 21 and uses the text block syntax. On an older release, drop the text block and concatenate the three strings instead.

```java
import java.util.regex.*;
import java.io.*;

public class LineCounter {

    private static final String PROMPT_TEXT = """
        Enter a string (multiline text, Enter to finish):
        """;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in))) {
            while (true) {
                System.out.print(PROMPT_TEXT);
                String input = reader.readAllLines().stream().collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.joining("\n"));

                if (input.isBlank()) {
                    System.out.println("The input is empty. Please enter some text.");
                    continue;
                }

                System.out.print("Enter a regular expression: ");
                String patternString = reader.readLine();
                if (patternString == null) {
                    break;
                }

                Pattern pattern;
                try {
                    pattern = Pattern.compile(patternString);
                } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
                    System.out.println("Invalid regular expression: " + e.getMessage());
                    continue;
                }

                Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(input);
                int count = 0;
                System.out.println("\nMatches:");
                while (matcher.find()) {
                    count++;
                    System.out.println("  Line " + matcher.startLine() + ", position " + matcher.start() + ": " + matcher.group());
                }
                System.out.println(count + " line" + (count == 1 ? "" : "s") + " matched.");
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The input method is where I am least sure. `readAllLines()` reads every line the user types until the stream closes, which means they have to finish the input with end-of-file, Ctrl+D or Ctrl+Z. People typing at a prompt usually expect to finish a line with Enter. A version that reads line by line, shows each line as it comes in, and stops when the user types a line containing only the word done is easier to use. I would build that version. The counting is the same in both. Here is the loop:

```java
List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
while (true) {
    System.out.print("Line: ");
    String line = reader.readLine();
    if (line == null || line.trim().equals("done")) {
        break;
    }
    lines.add(line);
}
String input = String.join("\n", lines);
```

On empty input the two versions differ. The one above asks again, which is what you asked for, and it is slower than printing zero. Whether it is the right behaviour is a real question. A user who typed nothing and hit Enter may simply have nothing to count. I lean toward asking once. I hold that lean loosely.

Here's a program that does this. The notes after it explain the design.

```python
import csv
import sys
from decimal import Decimal, InvalidOperation

ENCODING = "utf-8"
FILE = "data.csv"


def read_csv(path):
    """Read a CSV file and return (header, rows).

    The first row of the file becomes the header. Every other row
    becomes a data row, with its cell values converted as follows:
    integers, floating-point numbers, and Decimal values, in that order.
    Anything that won't convert becomes the empty string, and a
    message goes to the error log.

    If the file has no header row, the column names are NUM_0, NUM_1 and so on.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding=ENCODING) as f:
            reader = csv.reader(f)
            try:
                header = next(reader)
            except StopIteration:
                return [], []

            rows = []
            for row_num, row in enumerate(reader, start=2):
                converted = []
                for col_num, cell in enumerate(row):
                    cell = cell.strip()
                    value = convert_cell(cell)
                    converted.append(value)
                rows.append(converted)
            return header, rows
    except FileNotFoundError:
        sys.stderr.write(f"Error: the file '{path}' was not found.\n")
        raise
    except csv.Error as e:
        sys.stderr.write(f"Error: the file '{path}' is not a valid CSV. {e}\n")
        raise


def convert_cell(cell):
    """Convert a string to int, float or Decimal, or return an empty string."""
    if not cell:
        return ""
    try:
        return int(cell)
    except ValueError:
        pass
    try:
        return float(cell)
    except ValueError:
        pass
    try:
        return Decimal(cell)
    except InvalidOperation:
        pass
    return ""


def width_of(rows):
    """Return the width of each column, in characters.

    Each column is as wide as its widest cell, including the header.
    Empty cells count as zero.
    """
    if not rows:
        return [len(h) for h in header]
    col_widths = [0] * len(header)
    for row in rows:
        for i, cell in enumerate(row):
            cell_width = len(str(cell))
            if cell_width > col_widths[i]:
                col_widths[i] = cell_width
    for i, h in enumerate(header):
        if len(h) > col_widths[i]:
            col_widths[i] = len(h)
    return col_widths


def format_row(row, col_widths):
    """Format one data row as a string."""
    parts = []
    for i, cell in enumerate(row):
        cell = str(cell)
        padding = col_widths[i] - len(cell)
        parts.append(" " + cell + " " * padding)
    return "".join(parts)


def header_row(col_widths):
    """Format the header row, with a line of dashes under it."""
    parts = []
    for i, h in enumerate(header):
        cell = h.center(col_widths[i])
        parts.append("|" + cell + "|")
    result = "".join(parts)

    sep = []
    for w in col_widths:
        sep.append("-" * (w + 2))
    sep.append("\n")

    return result + "".join(sep)


def print_table(header, rows):
    if not header:
        sys.stderr.write("The file contains no header row.\n")
        return

    col_widths = width_of(rows)
    print(header_row(col_widths))
    for row in rows:
        print(format_row(row, col_widths))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    header, rows = read_csv(FILE)
    print_table(header, rows)
```

A sample file, "data.csv":

```
name,quantity,price
apples,12,3.45
bananas,,2.10
oranges,8,
grapes,4,12
```

This runs as `python table.py`. Change the file name by setting `FILE` at the top. Each row of the CSV becomes a row in the table, with the values in their own columns. Numbers that look like numbers come out as numbers. Blank cells stay blank.

The program counts from zero, and so does the file. The header is row zero. The first data row is row one. If a row has more cells than the header, the extra cells go into the table with no column name above them, and that row gets no warning. If it has fewer, the missing cells come out empty.

The error log collects everything that went wrong, in order, with the row number of each bad row. So if row four has a bad price and row eleven does too, you see both, and you see which is which. The program keeps going after a bad cell. It stops after a bad header.

The main thing to be careful about is rounding. Floating-point numbers can't hold most decimals exactly, and 0.1 plus 0.2 comes out as 0.30000000000000004. The `Decimal` type holds them exactly. Where a program has to round, it should say how, and this one doesn't round at all.

The other thing is the column width. The program finds it by looking at every cell in the file, twice, once to count and once to print. For a file of a few thousand rows that's nothing, well under a second. For a file of a few million it's a few seconds, which is fine and which you'd see.

What you'd want to change:

The width calculation has to read the whole file before it prints a line. If the table is huge, a streaming version would print the header, then each row as it reads it, and drop the column widths. Then nothing would be centred, and the right way to get the widths back is to measure each column as you go and print them once at the end. I'd do that only if the table gets big.
The currency symbols. If your file has "$" in the price column, they come out as text, next to the numbers, which is fine if nobody reads the table as figures.
The log goes to the standard error stream. Depending on how you run the program, that may go to your screen or to a log file, and you'd only find that out by checking.

Most of this is straightforward, and much of it is already built. A search bar with filters for subject, grade and price does about what it says. So does a calendar where parents can book their own slots, which saves the tutor's afternoons. A profile that lists a tutor's qualifications and how long they've taught is useful as far as it goes. Parents who need a tutor for next week will read it and trust much of it.

The reviews are where I'd want you to think twice. A rating is only worth anything if the person who wrote it actually sat through the lessons. Right now a parent can write one after a single meeting, or a tutor can write one about a student. If either happens often, the ratings stop telling parents anything, and the honest tutors pay for it, because their five stars get lost in the noise. So let only someone who has already paid for sessions leave a review, and let the tutor reply publicly to whatever they disagree with. Then nobody has to wonder who wrote what.

The same goes for the messaging. Parents and tutors should be able to talk freely about homework and schedules. But a parent who can message a tutor privately about anything, including sessions they haven't paid for, is also the way a review gets bought, one on one in a private chat. So keep the conversation open, and let the platform see it. A tutor who gets a review they think is unfair can appeal it to you, and a human should look at that appeal. Nobody here has a claim to secrecy.

Then there is the question of who is hiring whom. In most of these arrangements the tutor is the independent person. The platform is there for the parents, and it should say so, because a parent who thinks the platform works for the tutor may be getting the wrong advice about a bad match. The same goes for the fees. If the platform takes a cut, parents should see it in the price before they book.

One thing more. You can't really check a tutor's qualifications from outside. A degree on a profile is a claim, just like a five-star rating, and the parent has no way of knowing which it is. So put every claim to the person who issues it. A university's records office can confirm a degree in one afternoon. A parent phoning a former student to ask how things went is a fair thing to ask a tutor's references.

Here is the core of the search, which handles both desktop and mobile. On a small screen the filters sit above the results and drop down, so the list stays long enough to read. On a wide screen they sit to the side, and the search bar sits above both.

```html
<input type="search" id="search" placeholder="Search by subject, grade or tutor name">
<div id="filters" class="filters">
  <label>Subject
    <select id="subject">
      <option value="">Any subject</option>
      <option>Math</option><option>Science</option><option>History</option>
      <option>English</option><option>Music</option><option>Other</option>
    </select>
  </label>
  <label>Grade <select id="grade"><option>Primary</option><option>Secondary</option></select></label>
  <label>Price per session <select id="price"><option>Any</option><option>Under $20</option><option>$20 to $40</option><option>Over $40</option></select></label>
  <label><input type="checkbox" id="online"> Online only</label>
</div>
<div id="results" class="results"></div>

<script>
const tutors = [ /* from your database */ ];

function render() {
  const subject = document.getElementById('subject').value;
  const grade = document.getElementById('grade').value;
  const price = document.getElementById('price').value;
  const online = document.getElementById('online').checked;
  const query = document.getElementById('search').value.toLowerCase();

  const filtered = tutors.filter(t => {
    if (subject && t.subject !== subject) return false;
    if (grade && t.grade !== grade) return false;
    if (price) {
      if (price === 'Under $20' && t.price >= 20) return false;
      if (price === '$20 to $40' && (t.price < 20 || t.price > 40)) return false;
      if (price === 'Over $40' && t.price <= 40) return false;
    }
    if (online && !t.online) return false;
    if (query && !t.name.toLowerCase().includes(query) && !t.subject.includes(query)) return false;
    return true;
  });

  document.getElementById('results').innerHTML =
    filtered.map(t => `
      <div class="card">
        <h3>${t.name}</h3>
        <p>${t.subject}, grade ${t.grade}</p>
        <p>Qualifications: ${t.qualifications || 'Not listed'}</p>
        <p>Experience: ${t.years} years</p>
        <p>Price: $${t.price} per session</p>
        <p><a href="/tutor/${t.id}">Profile and reviews</a></p>
      </div>
    `).join('');
}

document.querySelectorAll('input, select').forEach(el => el.addEventListener('change', render));
document.getElementById('search').addEventListener('input', render);
</script>

